raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "How can researchers measure partisan division in online discussions at the level of a particular topic, region and time?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We introduce a methodological framework to reliably measure polarization in public discourse at fine-grained resolution,\nleveraging the vast quantity of human-generated text available on digital platforms.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "This regional heterogeneity necessitates studying polarization through a multidimensional lens that accounts for specific\nevents, topics, and geographic variation.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Across American states and Canadian provinces, how does polarization over lockdowns, masks and vaccines relate to regional political leanings, conspiracy-related discussion and vaccination rates?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically, we analyze millions of\ntweets to determine how polarization correlates with: (1) the ideological leanings of different regions; (2) exposure to conspiracy theory-related content; and (3) vaccination rates.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Our analysis focused on three specific pandemic-related topics: lockdowns, masks, and vaccines.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do peaks in daily polarization coincide with identifiable political or public health events, and how quickly do they subside?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In particular, we examined two types of events: 1) pre-selected political and\nvaccine-related events (Table 8, shown as vertical lines in Fig 13) and 2) highly polarized events detected through analysis of the highest peaks in polarization (Tables 9 and 10, shown as red circles in Fig 13).",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "To evaluate the overall responsiveness of our measure to polarizing events, we computed\naverage polarization scores across topics (Fig 14A,B) and performed event-triggered averaging.",
            "page": 19
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Comparing how partisans talk about the same health measure reveals divisions that a single national polarization score would miss: the gap varies by place, political leaning, topic and event.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "By analyzing millions of tweets and\ntracking semantic differences between partisan groups across different regions and topics, we have demonstrated that\npolarization is not uniform but varies significantly by geography, political leaning, and public health topic, with substantial\ndifferences between the two countries.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "The temporal patterns\nin our polarization metric successfully captured real-world political events, providing a powerful lens through which to\nunderstand how public discourse evolves in response to political messaging and policy decisions.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Online partisan division was more closely associated with subsequent health behavior in the United States than in Canada. The authors interpret that contrast as evidence that the political setting matters, not that online discourse has a uniform effect on vaccination.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "while both countries experienced social media\npolarization, the U.S. showed stronger correlations between polarization, conspiracy theories, and public health outcomes\nsuch as vaccination rates. This suggests that the impact of online polarization on public health behaviors may be mediated by broader political and institutional factors.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors sampled the real-time Twitter stream from October 9, 2020, to January 4, 2021. Filtering yielded about 12.6 million COVID-related tweets linked to the United States and 46.6 million linked to Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We used Twitter’s (X) official API to collect 1% of real-time tweets for the United States and\nCanada from October 9, 2020 to January 4, 2021.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "This process yielded 12,552,213 tweets from 2,657,355 users for the United States and 46,636,206 tweets from\n1,757,675 users for Canada that shared COVID-19-related content.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Political scientists labeled hashtags and reviewed sampled tweets. The authors combined those labels with country-specific language models to classify discussion by topic.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Two political scientists manually annotated this list with topic and relevance labels, narrowing it to 631 relevant\nhashtags.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We then domain-adapted RoBERTa-base models [41] on the COVID-19 tweets from each country’s dataset through\nself-supervised learning (predicting masked words within tweets), creating country-specific pre-trained language\nmodels.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Two political scientists independently reviewed each tweet to determine relevance, discarding\ntweets where consensus couldn’t be reached. We then fine-tuned the respective domain-adapted RoBERTa models to\nclassify by topics.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The conspiracy measure counted discussion, not belief: a tweet debunking a theory counted alongside one promoting it, and topic categories could overlap.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For\nconspiracy-related tweets, relevant indicates that the content pertains to COVID-19 conspiracy theories, including tweets\nthat either promote the conspiracy or debunk / refute it, i.e., any tweet discussing the theory in either context. A tweet\ncould belong to multiple topics.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors geolocated users from profile locations when two mapping services agreed. Regional user counts closely tracked census population counts in both countries.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We processed this free-form text field using Open Street Map and the ArcGIS API, which both return latitude and\nlongitude coordinates when a location is found. We established a clear geolocation when both APIs returned coordinates\nwithin one degree of each other",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "we successfully geolocated 757,601 U.S. users with a strong correlation of 0.98 (n = 52, p = 9.27e-35, CI=[0.96,\n0.99]) to official population census data, and 282,454 Canadian users with a strong correlation of 0.92 (n = 13, p = 6.20e06, CI=[0.76, 0.98]).",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Explicit partisanship in profiles supplied labels for a second classifier based on users’ posting activity. The comparison was between Democrats and Republicans in the United States and between two multiparty families in Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We first identified politically explicit users based on their profile descriptions, using partisan keywords for each country’s major parties.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "We then used these profile-based classifications as labels to train a classifier based on user activity\npatterns.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "For the United States, we identified supporters of the Democratic (left) and Republican (right) parties. For Canada, we\ngrouped users into left (Liberal Party, New Democratic Party, and Green Party) and right (Conservative Party and People’s\nParty) partisan families.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors checked inferred affiliations against regional election results in both countries and against individual voter records in five U.S. states and Washington, DC. The regional correlations were about 0.8; individual agreement with registration records was about 74%.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we calculated the correlation between the ratio of liberal to\nconservative-labeled users per region in our dataset compared to official election results, obtaining strong correlations of\n0.802 for the United States and 0.815 for Canada",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "we matched Twitter users with primary voter registration records available for five states (Ohio, New York,\nFlorida, Arkansas, North Carolina) and Washington DC.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Our profile classifier achieved 74.35% accuracy and our activity classifier achieved 73.35% accuracy when compared\nto these official records.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For each topic, the authors averaged each user’s tweet embeddings and used the C-index to measure how distinctly the resulting user vectors clustered by party. Repeated subsampling made the calculation feasible for large groups.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To obtain a user embedding for a given topic, we aggregate all\nof the user’s relevant tweet embeddings by averaging.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "We then measure polarization by quantifying how far apart the language of left and right-leaning users is positioned\nin this embedding space, using the C-index [42], a robust clustering measure.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Our algorithm (Algorithm 1) repeatedly samples a fraction of users, calculates the polarization index on this sample,\nand increases the sample size until the coefficient of variation falls below a specified threshold",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Regional comparisons used weekly polarization scores averaged over 12 weeks and compared with election vote margins. National time series used daily scores; the authors examined the content at the largest peaks and averaged trajectories around identified events.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Ranking of 1 signifies the highest average weekly polarization\nbetween October 11, 2020 to January 3, 2021 (12 weeks).",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "When examined against partisan voting patterns (using the 2020 U.S. presidential election and 2019 Canadian federal\nelection)",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "For each topic, we analyzed the two highest peaks, and inferred the content discussed on those peaks.",
            "page": 20
          },
          {
            "text": "Daily aggregated partisan polarization. For the U.S. (A, B) and Canada (C, D), polarization is aggregated over topic by averaging over the\nvalues. We show pandemic-related new cases and deaths in background for reference. Event-triggered average polarization for identified events are\nlisted in Table 9 and Table 10 respectively.",
            "page": 21
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Official epidemiological data supplied case and death counts. The vaccination comparison used regional coverage with at least two doses one year after the tweets, when vaccines had become widely available.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We used officially reported COVID-19 new cases and death data from each country.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We used officially reported vaccination rates from one year after our Twitter\ndata collection period, as COVID-19 vaccines were only created and approved at the very end of our initial data\ncollection. The vaccination rates represent those who obtained at least two doses.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Conservative regions tended to be more polarized, but the relationship depended on the country and the intervention. Republican vote share was significantly associated with polarization over masks and vaccines, not lockdowns; the Canadian vote-margin associations were not statistically significant.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "conservative states and provinces show higher levels of polarization compared\nto their liberal counterparts.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "We find\nstrong, significant correlations in the US between Republican vote share and polarization around masks and vaccines,\nthough not for lockdowns. For Canada, these associations are not statistically significant, partly due to the smaller number\nof regions analyzed.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Individual regions departed sharply from the partisan pattern. Liberal Delaware was highly polarized, while Arkansas, Alabama and Idaho showed little division. In Canada, Quebec had the most polarized vaccine discussion.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Delaware, a liberal state, exhibited unexpectedly high polarization",
            "page": 23
          },
          {
            "text": "several conservative states like Arkansas, Alabama, and Idaho showed low polarization levels, possibly due to more unified opposition to restrictive COVID-19 measures like mask mandates [68].",
            "page": 23
          },
          {
            "text": "Our results confirmed that Quebec had the highest\nlevel of partisan division over vaccines, alongside the highest reported COVID-19 incidence in Canada during the first\nand second pandemic waves [89].",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "U.S. states with more polarized vaccine discussions subsequently had lower vaccination rates (r = −0.77). Canada did not reproduce that inverse relationship: excluding Nunavut yielded a positive correlation across just 12 regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Using official CDC vaccination data from a comparable period after vaccines became\navailable (October 11, 2021 to January 3, 2022), we confirm a strong negative correlation (r = −0.77, p < 0.001) between\nvaccine polarization and vaccination rates.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "We remove Nunavut as an outlier because of its very small population. The correlation is 0.74 with CI = [0.31, 0.92] (n = 12, p = 0.004).",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Polarization often rose and fell within days of contentious events. For example, a U.S. vaccine-discussion peak followed Biden’s first dose, while Canada’s largest lockdown-discussion peak coincided with a Toronto protest.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This aggregate analysis (Fig\n14c) confirms a rapid, symmetric rise-and-decay profile around polarization peaks, occurring on timescales of days.",
            "page": 19
          },
          {
            "text": "The second-largest\npeak occurred on December 22, 2020, the day after Biden received his first COVID-19 vaccine [54].",
            "page": 18
          },
          {
            "text": "In Canada’s lockdown discourse (Fig 13B), the highest peak occurred on October 17, 2020, coinciding with a large\nToronto anti-mask protest against COVID-19 measures.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Conservatives discussed conspiracy theories more often than progressives in both countries. Yet higher daily conspiracy-discussion volume did not mean greater partisan separation: its correlation with aggregate polarization was slightly negative in the United States and not significant in Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "conservative partisans in both countries tweet conspiracy-related content at higher rates than progressive\npartisans.",
            "page": 21
          },
          {
            "text": "we find a small but significant negative correlation\nfor the United States, while no significant correlation was observed for Canada.",
            "page": 21
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "Semantic separation is a proxy, not a direct measure of political disagreement. Averaging posts by user can obscure sarcasm and indirect disagreement in individual tweets.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we measured partisan polarization only through the proxy of semantic similarity, which may in certain cases obscure signals not captured by semantic\nembedding representations.",
            "page": 24
          },
          {
            "text": "As a result, subtle rhetorical strategies such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement may be less apparent, while consistent linguistic cues, including negation, are preserved in the aggregated representation.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Canadian analysis does not resolve divisions among parties within the same political family. The activity classifier confused those parties, and supporters of the Bloc Québécois and other minor parties were excluded.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the F1-score was not\nsatisfactory as parties within the liberal (left) party family and conservative (right) party family was easily confused as\nshown in the confusion matrix in Table 5. Hence, we grouped the parties based on their partisan leaning.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "We also\nremoved supporters of other minor parties and the Bloc Quebecois.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Self-reported affiliation can be wrong, and voter records are not a perfect benchmark: registrations can lag current leanings, and name matching can miss users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, our classification into partisan groups relies\non self-reported information, which may not be entirely accurate.",
            "page": 24
          },
          {
            "text": "voter registrations may be outdated or not fully reflect\ncurrent partisan leanings expressed online. We note that name-based matching can miss users due to nicknames, misspellings, or common names;",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The findings concern politically engaged Twitter users, not the distribution of opinion among all Americans or Canadians.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Twitter users are more politically engaged and often more partisan than the general population, which means our results reflect dynamics within a vocal subset of online political discourse.",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The English-only sample underrepresents francophones who do not tweet in English, making Quebec’s high score particularly difficult to generalize.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Fourth, we restricted\nour analysis to English-language tweets, which in Canada means we primarily captured perspectives of either anglophones or bilingual francophones — a potential source of bias. For example, the high polarization observed in Quebec\nregarding COVID-19 measures may be influenced by this language limitation.",
            "page": 24
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Profile locations can be missing, imprecise or false. Bots can also distort the analysis by amplifying partisan language and making groups appear farther apart.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "self-reported Twitter locations may be incomplete, imprecise, or even intentionally false\n(including the rare influence of VPNs);",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Automated accounts can contribute disproportionately to partisan discourse by generating large volumes of ideologically\nslanted content or amplifying existing narratives, potentially inflating the apparent semantic distance between political\ngroups.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The two national samples were not selected in exactly the same way. U.S. search terms emphasized the election, whereas Canadian terms targeted provinces and COVID-19 more directly, potentially affecting the cross-country comparison.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The U.S. keyword\nlist was dominated by election-related terms, then filtered for COVID-19 terms. By contrast, the Canadian keyword list\nincorporated province names and explicit COVID-related identifiers, which likely offered stronger pandemic relevance.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "This asymmetry may have contributed to cross-national differences in patterns of observed polarization, and this should\nbe considered when interpreting the comparative results.",
            "page": 25
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Late-2020 tweets and later vaccination rates were not contemporaneous. Their association may have predictive value, but it does not establish that polarized discussion reduced uptake; education and ideology are among the possible alternative explanations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Seventh, there are temporal mismatches between our Twitter dataset (collected in late 2020) and regional vaccination rates, which occurred primarily in 2021. As a result, correlations between polarization in vaccine-related discussions\nand later vaccination uptake should be understood as suggestive rather than causative.",
            "page": 25
          },
          {
            "text": "While the exact causal mechanism remains\nunclear, factors such as education and political ideology, which also have strong geographic dependencies, likely played\nsignificant roles [ 60].",
            "page": 23
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The small number of Canadian regions and the different policy setting, including vaccine mandates, limit conclusions about the Canadian vaccination correlation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While we get strong positive correlation with vaccination rate,\nit is over a relatively low number of points. In Canada, vaccines were mandated, requiring vaccine passports to be\nserved in public areas.",
            "page": 26
          },
          {
            "text": "However, with the few points, we do not have\na definite conclusion for this result.",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study identifies coincidences between events and peaks, not proof that a particular speech, protest or policy caused a peak. Nor does it establish a single explanation for greater division in conservative states.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While direct causal evidence is not available, we find that many of the largest polarization peaks coincide with\nhighly contentious events specific to each country’s context.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "While our analysis did not identify a single explanation for the relationship between conservative politics and higher\npolarization, state-specific trajectories provide insights.",
            "page": 23
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors do not quantify how much cross-border information flows themselves contributed to polarization or subsequent health outcomes.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, the role of cross-border\ninformation flows in shaping polarization deserves further investigation",
            "page": 26
          },
          {
            "text": "quantifying\ntheir precise impact on polarization and subsequent public health outcomes remains an important challenge for future\nwork.",
            "page": 26
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend computational approaches that infer polarization from language. They use semantic embeddings rather than requiring follower or retweet networks, a choice motivated by restrictions on access to network data rather than a rejection of network methods.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Recent computational approaches increasingly draw on text embedding methods to study polarization, using contextualized representations to detect partisan divides across topics and communities [34–36].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "However, access to user- and\nnetwork-level data has become increasingly restricted, particularly under recent platform policy changes. Consequently,\nour study adopts a strictly text-based method, which remains both accessible and scalable for analyzing large-scale digital\ndiscourse.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "They qualify the inference from linguistic difference to political division: groups may simply have different vocabularies. Their proposed comparison holds the contentious topic constant so that a semantic gap is more likely to reflect a difference in how partisans understand it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Therefore, dissimilar language alone does not necessarily indicate polarized\npositions.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "However, when examining discussions on the same contentious topic, language dissimilarity between different partisan\ngroups more likely reflects alternative semantic understandings of that specific topic—a phenomenon we hypothesize\ncorrelates strongly with polarization.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "They extend research on partisan responses to COVID-19 by moving from an overall association with conservatism to comparisons of particular places and changes around particular events.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In line with previous research ((e.g., [17,58–60])), we found that more right leaning states and provinces exhibited\ngreater partisan divisions around COVID-19 on Twitter (X), particularly concerning mask mandates and vaccine\ndistribution.",
            "page": 22
          },
          {
            "text": "However, we extended beyond these studies to characterize the geographic heterogeneity and temporal\nevolution of polarization, connecting features in our polarization metric to real-world events.",
            "page": 22
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Merkley and colleagues found unusual agreement across Canadian party lines on early emergency measures. Yang and colleagues qualify the reach of that finding: the agreement did not carry over to the social media discussions they examine.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Indeed, there was a rare “crosspartisan consensus” among Canadians regarding emergency measures in the early stages of the pandemic [103]. This\nconsensus, however, was not mirrored on social media, where conspiracy theories widely circulated [24,95].",
            "page": 24
          },
          {
            "text": "Merkley E, Bridgman A, Loewen PJ, Owen T, Ruths D, Zhilin O. A rare moment of cross-partisan consensus: Elite and public response to the\nCOVID-19 pandemic in Canada.",
            "page": 31
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
